course: dictionary
lesson: 15622
title: Tectonics: the Earth's moving pieces
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words start in one subject, such as science, and then get used in everyday talk too. Tectonics is one of these. When you meet a new word, look at the words around it, because they are its context and they leave clues.
explain: «Tectonic plates move a few centimetres a year, about as fast as your fingernails grow.» The sentence tells you that plates are big pieces that move very slowly. So tectonics must have something to do with these moving pieces of the Earth.
explain: The word can also be used in a wider way. «The new law caused a tectonic shift in how the country was run.» Nothing is moving under the ground here. A tectonic shift is a huge change. Check by swapping your meaning into the sentence: does "a huge change" still make sense? It does.
fact: Tectonics is the study of how the Earth's outer shell moves, and the idea that it is broken into huge pieces called plates.
fact: «Tectonic plates» move only a few centimetres a year, about as fast as your fingernails grow.
fact: Where plates meet there can be earthquakes, volcanoes and mountains, such as the Himalayas and the Andes.
fact: New Zealand and Iceland sit on plate boundaries, and the idea grew from Alfred Wegener's «continental drift» in 1912.
fact: Tectonics can also mean a big shift or change in politics or society; this is a formal, figurative use.
exercise: «Tectonic plates move a few centimetres a year.» How fast do the plates move? | As fast as a car | *A few centimetres a year | Not at all | A few kilometres a minute
exercise: «The new law caused a tectonic shift in how the country was run.» What does tectonic shift mean here? | *A huge change | A quick walk around the park | A map of the sea | A small tremor under a road
exercise: Where do many earthquakes and volcanoes happen, according to the lesson? | Only under the sea, far from land | Only on the highest mountains of the world | *Where tectonic plates meet | Only in the middle of big cities
test: Which phrase names the idea that the Earth's outer shell is broken into moving plates? | Plate lunch | *Plate tectonics | Tectonic weather | Tectonic cooking
test: In which year did the idea of «continental drift» come from Alfred Wegener? | 2012 | 1966 | *1912 | 1066
exam: In science, tectonics is about | the sounds that animals make | *the Earth's outer shell and its moving plates | how to bake bread | the names of ancient kings
